    "notes": [
      "Trades 2 and 3 run to step 05b, not 05: the step order is 4, 5, 5b, so stopping at 5 would skip the Domain B check entirely. Both domains must confirm the ledger holds exactly one record for the shared trade id.",
      "They are EXPECTED TO PASS both steps. Because all three share a trade id, a duplicate still finds trade 1 on the ledger; what proves the duplicate was rejected is test_trade_appears_exactly_once, which fails if a second record carries that id.",
      "Three trades with exactly the same values, injected one at a time so the simulator is called three times.",
      "identifiers \"shared\" is what makes them duplicates rather than three lookalike trades: the runner generates ONE tradeId, tradeSideId, uniqueTransactionId and pair of party references, and all three trades carry it.",
      "The identity is generated per run, not pinned in this file. Literal ids here would make every run a duplicate of the previous run rather than of its own first trade.",
      "Trade 1 is the original and runs the full pipeline. Every step must genuinely pass.",
      "Party and counterparty are the mixed HK/NY pair the sheet gives this row.",
      "tradeReference, tradeReferenceSupplement and counterpartyReferenceNumber are the shared identity, so all three copies carry identical references.",
      "ccpAlias, clearingBroker and counterpartyClearingBroker are blank and have no wrapper field, so they are left out of the payload.",
      "Party CCP and Cpty CCP are legal-entity reference data, not trade fields, so they are recorded here only."
    ]
